\paragraph{Stage 1: Anisotropy Decompression (Gen~0$\to$1).}
The pretrained Pythia-410M checkpoint exhibits high representational anisotropy: the leading singular value $\sigma_1 = 1942.8$ dominates the spectrum, yielding a compressed effective rank of $\rho = 417.55$.  Fine-tuning on synthetic data redistributes variance across dimensions, reducing $\sigma_1$ to 804.0 (a 58.6\% decrease) and raising the effective rank to 491.77 (+17.8\%).  The spectral log-volume increases correspondingly from 2366.68 to 2464.76, confirming that the representational volume expands as anisotropy dissipates.  This transition reflects a standard fine-tuning effect: adaptation to any downstream corpus disperses the pretrained model's dominant singular directions~\cite{biderman2023pythia}.  Crucially, this is not model collapse but rather a structural reorganization of the representation space.  For this reason, we designate Generation~1 as the baseline for all subsequent collapse measurements, excluding the pretrained-to-fine-tuned shift from the analysis.

\paragraph{Stage 2: Decompression Peak (Gen~1$\to$2).}
The spectral decompression continues into Generation~2 but at a sharply reduced rate.  Effective rank reaches its maximum at 496.44 (+0.95\% from Gen~1), and the spectral log-volume peaks at 2469.93.  The leading singular value continues to decline from 804.0 to 712.0, indicating ongoing but decelerating spectral equalization.  The near-zero growth rate signals that the decompression process is exhausting itself: the representation has reached its maximum uniformity under recursive synthetic training.

\paragraph{Stage 3: Sustained Collapse (Gen~3$\to$7).}
Both spectral indicators reverse direction at Generation~3 and decline monotonically through Generation~7.  The effective rank drops from its peak of 496.44 to 493.31, 485.67, 479.21, 473.00, and 464.20 at Generations~3--7 respectively, a cumulative decline of $-6.5\%$ from peak.  The spectral log-volume falls from 2469.93 to 2440.57, 2405.05, 2370.17, 2335.17, and 2304.19, a steeper cumulative decline of $-6.7\%$.  The per-generation rate of effective rank decline accelerates: $-0.6\%$ (Gen~2$\to$3), $-1.5\%$ (Gen~3$\to$4), $-1.3\%$ (Gen~4$\to$5), $-1.3\%$ (Gen~5$\to$6), and $-1.9\%$ (Gen~6$\to$7).  This acceleration confirms that spectral collapse is a self-reinforcing process rather than a transient perturbation.

