The functional metrics exhibit monotonic degradation from the first recursive generation through Generation~7, with no sign of stabilization.  Perplexity rises from 11.38 at Generation~1 to 38.89 at Generation~7, a 242\% increase that reflects the model's accelerating inability to predict real-world text distributions.  The per-generation perplexity increment grows from 2.20 (Gen~1$\rightarrow$2) to 2.67 (Gen~2$\rightarrow$3) to 3.55 (Gen~3$\rightarrow$4) to 4.79 (Gen~4$\rightarrow$5) to 6.32 (Gen~5$\rightarrow$6) to 7.97 (Gen~6$\rightarrow$7), confirming super-linear degradation.  Distinct-4 declines from 0.6256 to 0.4474 ($-28.5\%$ from Gen~1), indicating that the model's output vocabulary contracts as it trains on its own increasingly homogeneous samples.

HellaSwag accuracy provides downstream task evidence for representational collapse.  Accuracy drops continuously from 0.4130 (pretrained) through 0.3897 (Gen~1) to 0.3187 (Gen~7), a total decline of 22.8\% from the pretrained checkpoint and 18.2\% from the Gen~1 baseline.  This confirms that spectral collapse is not merely an embedding-space phenomenon but propagates to downstream commonsense reasoning capability.  The HellaSwag trajectory parallels the spectral indicators with no lag, declining monotonically at every generation.

The training loss trajectory reveals a characteristic divergence from generalization metrics.  Loss decreases monotonically from 1.623 (Gen~1) to 1.015 (Gen~7), while perplexity on held-out real data increases by 242\% over the same interval.  This pattern is the hallmark of distributional narrowing: as the synthetic training distribution loses diversity across generations, the model memorizes it with less effort.  Lower training loss in this context is a symptom of overfitting to synthetic distributions rather than improved generalization.  The widening gap between training loss and evaluation perplexity at each generation provides an independent early-warning signal of collapse that requires no spectral computation.

A critical temporal asymmetry emerges from comparing the spectral and functional indicators.  Functional metrics (perplexity, HellaSwag) decline from Generation~1 onward, while spectral indicators peak at Generation~2 and reverse at Generation~3, revealing a two-generation detection delay when using spectral inflection as the warning signal.  This lag does not diminish the utility of spectral early-warning signals; rather, it highlights their complementary role.  Spectral metrics offer two practical advantages over functional indicators: (1)~they require no text generation, labeled benchmarks, or external evaluation sets, only a single forward pass through a small unlabeled probe set ($\sim$1K sequences), and (2)~they provide a geometric interpretation of the collapse mechanism (loss of representational volume) rather than merely measuring downstream symptoms.  These properties make spectral signals suitable for closed-loop control, where evaluation cost and latency are critical constraints.
